WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.880
A man sits in his studio with his head in his&nbsp;
hands. In front of him stands an almost empty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:27.040
canvas, and the cold light of day comes in through&nbsp;
the window, while a small stove is still burning.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.320
Behind him, we see a white, transparent figure&nbsp;
leaning over his shoulder, as if whispering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:38.400
something into his ear. And in the shadows,&nbsp;
next to the canvas, a skull watches in silence.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.840
The scene is already quite mysterious, because we&nbsp;
do not know what this poor man might be hearing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:49.120
the middle of his loneliness. But when we look to&nbsp;
the right and discover that there is a weapon on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:55.440
the table, painted in an almost imperceptible&nbsp;
way, the work becomes truly disturbing.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.000
This painting was created more than&nbsp;
a hundred years ago, and even today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.480
we know almost nothing about who&nbsp;
painted it or what it truly means.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:10.240
At the bottom of the canvas, we can read the&nbsp;
signature of a painter named Adolf Werner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:16.160
However, there is practically no information&nbsp;
about this man, and even less about this painting.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:22.640
The only thing we know is that in 2024 it was&nbsp;
sold at auction, and an anonymous buyer paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:29.200
7,500 dollars for it. Thanks to this, we know&nbsp;
that it is now part of a private collection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.880
and that it may never be exhibited in public.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.800
This work is known as The Misery, or by&nbsp;
its original German title, Das Elend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.840
and according to some sources,&nbsp;
it may have been painted in 1909.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.760
As I mentioned a moment ago, there&nbsp;
is almost no information about its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:53.360
author. Some sources describe him&nbsp;
as an Austrian painter born in 1862,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.760
while others present him as a German&nbsp;
artist from an earlier generation.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:03.600
For this very reason, to this day there is no&nbsp;
academic study that has analyzed this painting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.920
So what we are going to do in this video is&nbsp;
something that has rarely been done with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.760
painting, and that is to give it an in-depth&nbsp;
interpretation based on what we can see in it.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.920
Let us start with the man.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.920
This mysterious man is sitting on a&nbsp;
chair, with his elbows resting on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.240
knees and his hands pressing against&nbsp;
his head. We know he is not working,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.160
because his gaze is not directed at&nbsp;
the canvas he was probably painting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:34.000
just moments ago. Instead, his eyes are&nbsp;
lost, fixed on some point on the floor.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.960
This is probably the reason for his anguish.&nbsp;
Behind him, we can see a canvas that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:44.960
practically blank, barely marked by a few strokes.&nbsp;
For someone who spends their days painting, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.640
is no image more distressing than that, because&nbsp;
it is proof that something inside him has stopped.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:56.400
But that is not all. If we look at the rest of&nbsp;
the studio, everything seems abandoned. A dried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.840
plant hangs above the easel. A framed painting&nbsp;
fades into the shadows. And everything makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:08.160
this place look like the space of someone who&nbsp;
once created, and who now can no longer do so.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.440
But now let us look at what the man cannot see.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.480
Behind him there is a figure&nbsp;
wrapped in a white veil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.480
practically transparent. Through its&nbsp;
body we can see the chair and the wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.520
which tells us it is not a real person, but&nbsp;
a spirit. This figure is leaning over him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.720
with its face pressed against his head, and&nbsp;
it seems as if it were speaking into his ear.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.560
But the most unsettling thing is&nbsp;
not what it seems to be telling him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.960
but what it seems to be pointing at.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:40.400
If we follow the direction of its arm, we see&nbsp;
that it extends toward the table on the right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.880
And that is where the most disturbing&nbsp;
detail of the entire painting appears.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:50.800
On the table there is a small metallic gleam,&nbsp;
painted so subtly that most people do not notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:56.960
it at first glance. But we only need to look a&nbsp;
little closer to discover that it is a weapon.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.040
It seems that the spirit is not only&nbsp;
whispering into this poor man’s ear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.000
but also pointing him toward the weapon. As if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.800
were quietly suggesting that there is a&nbsp;
way to put an end to all his suffering.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.760
If we think about it, this is an extremely&nbsp;
cruel scene. With his head in his hands and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.480
his gaze lost on the floor, it is clear&nbsp;
that the man cannot see the spirit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.200
nor the gesture it is making. And&nbsp;
yet, he does seem to hear its voice.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.880
But curiously, this is not&nbsp;
the only presence in the room.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.320
In the shadows between the window and the easel,&nbsp;
we can see a skull emerging from the darkness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.120
And unlike the white spirit, this figure&nbsp;
does not seem to be speaking or pointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.200
at anything in particular. It&nbsp;
simply seems to be waiting.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.480
We could think that these two figures represent&nbsp;
two different things. The spirit by his side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.920
could be read as temptation, whispering to him&nbsp;
and pushing him toward the weapon. And the skull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:58.160
would be Death itself, patient, watching from the&nbsp;
shadows to see what decision this man will make.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.440
But there is one last detail that could change&nbsp;
the meaning of the entire scene, and one we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.720
not talked about yet. And that is the stove&nbsp;
that is still burning beneath the window.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.720
But before we talk about it, I would like&nbsp;
to remind you that if you would like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.800
have this work in your collection,&nbsp;
you can now do so from our website.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.080
In the link in the description you will find our&nbsp;
catalog with more than 1,000 carefully selected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.800
works, so you can choose your favorites&nbsp;
and begin your own art collection at home.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.880
Shipping is free worldwide, and by&nbsp;
purchasing from the store, you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.760
be supporting me enormously in continuing&nbsp;
to create videos like this every week.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.920
Now then, let us continue...

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.160
If we look at the left side of the&nbsp;
composition, we see that on the windowsill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.520
a small stove is still burning. Its fire&nbsp;
is the only warm light in the entire room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:53.200
standing out among the grays, the blacks, and the&nbsp;
whites of the color palette chosen by the artist.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.120
We could interpret this detail in&nbsp;
several ways. Perhaps it is simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.680
the stove that heats a cold studio.&nbsp;
But in a painting so full of symbols,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.720
it is hard not to see it as something more,&nbsp;
so it could be read as the last flame that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.800
is still alive. A spark of life, or of&nbsp;
creativity, that has not yet gone out.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.680
Something else to keep in mind is its position.&nbsp;
The most observant among you will have already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.880
noticed that the stove is on the same side as&nbsp;
the window. The same window that opens onto the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:28.400
light of day, letting us see that outside,&nbsp;
the world goes on, while inside this room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:33.440
a man is trapped between his thoughts and&nbsp;
the voice pointing him toward the weapon.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:37.920
The mystery of this painting does not lie so&nbsp;
much in the words this man might be hearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.640
but in the decision he is about to make.&nbsp;
Because the artist who painted this work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:48.480
left us all the clues, but he does not tell&nbsp;
us how this man’s story ends. We do not know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:53.440
whether he will give in to the voice and reach&nbsp;
for the weapon, or whether, on the contrary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:59.840
he will be able to escape his thoughts and step&nbsp;
out into the world outside, where life goes on.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:04.000
Now, the idea of painting Death&nbsp;
accompanying an artist in his studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.440
was not new at the time. As we have seen in&nbsp;
other videos, Arnold Böcklin also painted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:15.280
self-portrait in which a skeleton plays&nbsp;
the violin next to his ear. And in 1896,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.800
Lovis Corinth portrayed himself beside a&nbsp;
skeleton hanging in his studio in Munich.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.920
However, there is an important difference&nbsp;
between these paintings. In those works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.400
Death is a reminder that life ends&nbsp;
for everyone, while in The Misery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.920
Death does not come alone, but accompanied by&nbsp;
someone who is trying to hasten its arrival.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.320
And perhaps that is why this painting&nbsp;
is so disturbing. Because it does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.560
speak of death as a destiny, but&nbsp;
of the temptation to reach it.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.760
I would love for you to leave&nbsp;
me your interpretation of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.200
painting in the comments. What do&nbsp;
you think the two figures in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:56.229
room represent? And what do you think&nbsp;
that small burning stove could mean?

00:07:56.229 --> 00:08:02.880
As always, thank you for watching the video until&nbsp;
the end, and I will see you in the next video.

